---
title: Spaces
description: The unit of end-to-end encrypted sharing and sync — space keys, epochs, members, signed envelopes, and the SDK surface for creating, sharing and rotating a space.
---

A **space** is the unit of sharing and sync for end-to-end encrypted records. Every encrypted
row belongs to exactly one space, every space has one symmetric **space key**, and membership
of a space is what grants a user the ability to decrypt its rows at all.

The server stores wrapped keys and opaque ciphertext envelopes. It never holds a space key, and
it cannot read, filter or search the rows inside a space. Everything below happens in the
[Vault](/docs/neoworks/encryption) — a sandboxed origin your app embeds but never reads keys
from.

## What a space is

| Property | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `space_id` | Client-minted UUID. Chosen before upload so signatures can bind it. |
| `collection` | Which entity family the space holds — `contacts` today. Keys are per collection. |
| `kind` | `personal`, `shared` or `adhoc`. |
| `key_epoch` | Current key generation. Starts at 1, increments on every rotation. |
| `seq` | Monotonic per-space write counter. Drives the sync cursor. |
| `purge_horizon` | Seq below which tombstoned rows have been reaped. Pulling from under it forces a full resync. |

Three kinds, one mechanism:

- **`personal`** — the user's own space, created on first run. Server-side creation is
  idempotent per collection, so two devices racing produce one space, not two.
- **`shared`** — a long-lived space with named members. This is "a shared address book".
- **`adhoc`** — same machinery, meant for short-lived sharing of a small set of rows.

A space is not a folder. Rows do not move between spaces implicitly: which space you write to
is chosen at write time, and re-sharing a row means writing it into the target space.

## Key hierarchy

```
Account Master Key (AMK)          — never leaves the Vault
  ├── scope keypair (X25519)      — per collection, e.g. "contacts"
  │     └── receives sealed space keys (crypto_box_seal)
  ├── signing keypair (Ed25519)   — blake2b(AMK, "nw-space-signing-v1")
  │     └── signs envelopes and wrapped keys; same identity on every device
  └── (per space)
        space key (32 random bytes, per epoch)
          └── row key = blake2b(space_key, "nw-space-item:" + item_id)
                └── AES-256-GCM over the row payload
```

Four things follow from this shape:

1. **No stored per-row keys.** The row key is derived on demand from the space key and the item
   id, so a space of 10 000 contacts still stores exactly one key per epoch per member.
2. **Sharing costs one seal per member, not one re-encryption per row.** Inviting someone seals
   the existing space key to their scope public key; the ciphertext is untouched.
3. **The signing identity is account-level, not device-level.** Every device derives the same
   Ed25519 key from the AMK, so peers pin one signer key per user rather than tracking devices.
4. **Scope keypairs are per collection.** A client granted `contacts:*` receives contacts space
   keys and nothing else, even if the same user has spaces in other collections.

## Members, roles and wrapped keys

A membership row carries the caller's own view of the space:

```ts
{
  space: { space_id, owner_id, collection, kind, key_epoch, seq, purge_horizon },
  member: {
    user_id,
    role: 'owner' | 'writer' | 'reader',
    status: 'invited' | 'active',
    added_by_id,
    wrapped_keys: [{ epoch, wrapped_key, signature }],
    accept_signature
  }
}
```

`wrapped_keys` holds **one entry per epoch the member is allowed to read** — not just the
current one. Old epochs are kept so a member can still decrypt rows that have not been
re-encrypted yet (see [rotation](#rotation-and-lazy-re-encryption)). A single
`GET /api/v1/spaces?collection=…` therefore bootstraps every key the client needs.

`role` is enforced server-side on writes: a `reader` pushing an envelope gets `403`.

### Wrap signatures and pinning

Each wrapped key is signed by the inviter over

```
"nw-space-key-v1" | space_id | member_user_id | epoch | blake2b(space_key)
```

The context includes a fingerprint of the space key itself, so a valid signature proves the
signer actually held the key. The server cannot substitute key material and re-sign it.

On unlock, the recipient's Vault verifies that signature against the inviter's published
signing key and **pins** it. A later wrap that verifies against a different signer, or fails to
verify at all, comes back as `ok: false` from `unlockAll()`:

> **Treat a pin mismatch as fatal for that space.** It means the key material for a space you
> already trusted changed underneath you. Surface it to the user and stop syncing that space —
> do not silently re-pin.

Accepting an invite uploads the recipient's own re-signature over the same context
(`accept_signature`), which is what makes acceptance verifiable to the other members rather
than a server-asserted flag.

## Envelopes

A row on the wire is an **envelope**: a header the server can read, plus a blob it cannot.

```
item_id, space_id, seq, key_epoch, schema_ver,
deleted, author_id, created_at, updated_at   ← server-visible header
blob                                          ← AES-256-GCM ciphertext
sig                                           ← Ed25519, author's signing key
```

The GCM **additional authenticated data** binds the header to the ciphertext:

```
"nw-space-item-v1" | item_id | space_id | collection | key_epoch | schema_ver | base_seq | deleted
```

That binding is what stops the server from splicing a valid blob into a different row, space or
epoch — any such move changes the AAD and decryption fails.

Two details worth knowing:

- The AAD binds **`base_seq`**, not `seq`. The client encrypts before the server assigns a
  sequence number, so the envelope commits to the version it was written *against*.
- `deleted` is inside the AAD, so a tombstone cannot be resurrected by replaying an older blob
  with the flag flipped.

`sig` is Ed25519 over `blake2b(AAD ‖ blob)`. A tombstone signs the AAD with an empty blob.
Readers verify it against the author's published signing key, fetched from the key directory
(`GET /api/v1/keys/public?scope=…&user=…`) and cached.

### Two kinds of delete

- **Soft delete** — a flag *inside* the encrypted payload. The row still syncs, still has
  history, and can be restored. This is what a user's "trash" means. The server cannot tell a
  trashed row from an active one.
- **Envelope tombstone** — `deleted: true` on the header. The server drops the ciphertext,
  peers purge the row locally, and after the retention window the row falls below
  `purge_horizon`. Irreversible.

## Sync

Sync is per space, cursor-based on `seq`.

**Pull** — `GET /api/v1/spaces/{id}/items?since={cursor}&limit={n}` returns envelopes with
`seq > since`, ordered by `seq` (default limit 200, max 500). The client decrypts, verifies the
signature, and advances its cursor.

**Push** — `PUT /api/v1/spaces/{id}/items/{item}` with `base_seq` as the optimistic-concurrency
token. Three outcomes:

| Response | Meaning | What the client does |
|---|---|---|
| `200 { seq, key_epoch }` | Accepted | Advance the local row's seq, drop the outbox entry |
| `409 { error: "conflict", current }` | Someone else wrote first | Decrypt `current`, merge on device, retry against the new seq |
| `409 { error: "stale_epoch", current_epoch }` | Key rotated underneath the write | Re-encrypt at the new epoch, retry |
| `410` | Cursor below `purge_horizon` | Wipe the local space and resync from 0 |

Merging happens on the device, in plaintext, because the server cannot see field values. Batch
import uses `POST /api/v1/spaces/{id}/items/batch` (1–500 items, one transaction each, so a
single conflict does not fail the import).

**History** — `GET /api/v1/spaces/{id}/items/{item}/versions` returns the encrypted version
chain oldest first; each entry is decrypted client-side like any other envelope.

## Rotation and lazy re-encryption

Removing a member is two steps, and the second is not optional:

1. `DELETE /api/v1/spaces/{id}/members/{user}` removes the membership and answers
   `{ rotation_required: true, key_epoch }`.
2. The owner's Vault mints epoch `key_epoch + 1`, rewraps it for every remaining member, and
   uploads the rewraps with `expected_epoch` as an assert. A concurrent rotation loses with
   `409` — refetch and retry.

Existing rows are **not** re-encrypted eagerly. They keep their old epoch and are re-encrypted
opportunistically as they are next written. `GET …/items?stale_epoch=true` returns the worklist
of rows still below the current epoch, so a client can drain it in the background.

> Rotation protects **future** writes. A removed member who already synced a row still has that
> plaintext copy — nothing can retract it. Rotation guarantees they cannot read anything
> written after removal.

## Using spaces from the SDK

Configure the SDK once, then take a `SpacesResource` per collection. It needs your own bare
user id, because wrap signatures bind the member they are addressed to.

```ts
import { sdk, type SpacesResource } from '@neoworks-dev/sdk';

sdk.config({
  url: OAUTH_URL,
  apiUrl: API_URL,
  clientId: CLIENT_ID,
  redirectUri: `${location.origin}/callback`,
  scopes: ['contacts:read', 'contacts:write']
});

const spaces: SpacesResource = sdk.spaces('contacts', ownUserId);
```

`ownUserId` is the `sub` claim of the access token.

### Listing and unlocking

```ts
// Every membership for this collection, including the caller's wrapped keys.
const memberships = await spaces.list();

// Unwrap + pin all keys, auto-accept fresh invites. Call before syncing.
const results = await spaces.unlockAll();
for (const result of results) {
  if (!result.ok) {
    // Pin mismatch or unsealing failure — do not sync this space.
    console.error(result.spaceId, result.error);
  }
}
```

For encrypted **contacts** you do not call this yourself: `sdk.contacts` unlocks its spaces on
the first data op. Use `SpacesResource` directly when you are building sharing UI.

### Creating a space

```ts
// Personal space, created on first run and idempotent across devices.
const personal = await spaces.ensurePersonal();

// A shared space. `nameEnc` is a client-encrypted label — the server never sees the name.
const shared = await spaces.create('shared', nameEnc);
```

### Sharing

```ts
// Look up by user id or email; the SDK fetches their scope public key and
// wraps every epoch the caller holds.
await spaces.invite(spaceId, { email: 'ada@example.com' }, 'writer');

// Roles: 'writer' | 'reader'. The owner role is only ever the creator's.
await spaces.invite(spaceId, { userId }, 'reader');
```

The invitee's client accepts implicitly on its next `unlockAll()`.

### Removing a member

```ts
// Removes the membership and immediately rotates the key for everyone left.
await spaces.removeMember(spaceId, userId);
```

Do not call `DELETE …/members/{user}` directly unless you then rotate — `removeMember()` exists
precisely so the two cannot drift apart.

### Inspecting and verifying

```ts
const { space, members } = await spaces.getDetail(spaceId);

// Re-verify every wrap against the Vault's pin store, no side effects.
const pins = await spaces.verifyPins();

await spaces.deleteSpace(spaceId);
```

## REST reference

All routes take `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and are scoped to the collection the space
belongs to.

| Method | Path | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `POST` | `/api/v1/spaces` | Create a space with the creator's self-wrap |
| `GET` | `/api/v1/spaces?collection=` | Caller's memberships + wrapped keys |
| `GET` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}` | Space plus its member list |
| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}` | Delete a space |
| `POST` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/members` | Invite a member with per-epoch wraps |
| `POST` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/accept` | Accept an invite with a pin re-signature |
| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/members/{user}` | Remove a member (rotation required after) |
| `POST` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/rotate` | Bump the epoch with rewraps for remaining members |
| `GET` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/items` | Pull envelopes after a cursor |
| `PUT` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/items/{item}` | Push one envelope (OCC on `base_seq`) |
| `POST` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/items/batch` | Push 1–500 envelopes |
| `GET` | `/api/v1/spaces/{id}/items/{item}/versions` | Encrypted version history |
| `GET` | `/api/v1/keys/public?scope=&user=` | Key directory: scope public key + signing key |

## What the server can and cannot see

**Can see:** space ids, collection, kind, membership graph, roles, epoch numbers, sequence
numbers, row ids, blob sizes, write timestamps, tombstone flags, and who authored each write.

**Cannot see:** space keys, row keys, any field of any row, space names, or which rows relate to
which. It cannot filter, sort or search encrypted rows — all of that runs client-side inside the
Vault against decrypted data.

Metadata is a real disclosure. A space with three members and daily writes tells the server
that three people collaborate daily, just not on what.

